<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Adaptive launcher icon.

  Both layers are raster (PNG density buckets in mipmap-*/), not vector drawables, and that is a
  compatibility decision rather than an aesthetic one:

    * Android's own contract is that <background>/<foreground> are drawables, and vectors are
      legal there. In practice, OEM launchers — Honor/MagicOS and the other EMUI-derived ones in
      particular — rasterise the layers through their own pipeline, and a layer supplied as a
      <vector> is not always recognised as "an adaptive icon this app provides". When that check
      fails the launcher falls back to decorating the legacy bitmap: it rounds the corners of the
      flat icon and paints a ring/backing behind it. That is the "circled with a halo" appearance
      a vector-only icon picks up on those devices while neighbouring apps look correct.
    * Shipping the same artwork as PNG in every density bucket removes that branch entirely: it
      is the exact shape Android Studio's Image Asset wizard produces, and the shape every
      launcher is tested against.
    * mipmap-anydpi-v26 is the highest-priority bucket and wins on every device with minSdk 26,
      so this file is always the one used; the PNGs are the layers it points at.

  Geometry note: the card and its blocks sit at 29..79 / 30..78 in the 108x108 canvas, i.e.
  inside the 72x72 safe zone (18..90), so no launcher mask can clip the artwork.

  <monochrome> points at its own PNG rather than reusing the foreground. Some engines mis-handle
  one resource being referenced twice by the same icon, and a themed icon wants a flat
  silhouette anyway — the blue blocks are colour that the system is going to discard.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
